  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.104pt, 152.3pt, "The microstructure of an AM material depends on the shape of the built component, ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 172.94pt, "because the local cooling and reheating conditions, which are related to the component ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 193.7pt, "shape, can change the microstructure.  ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 222.38pt, "This is very obvious in the strut cross-sections in the AlSi10Mg lattice structures ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 243.02pt, "(Section 3.2.2), which have completely different microstructures from the (nominally) ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 263.78pt, "larger parts of the same AM build, and which changed with the three-dimensional ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 284.45pt, "geometry of the surroundings. The grain size and any microstructural features in these ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 305.21pt, "regions apply only to regions with the same local geometry, including distance from ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 325.85pt, "nearby thermal masses. Since each strut section contains too few grains to build up a ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 346.61pt, "grain size distribution, multiple similar samples would be necessary to measure the ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 367.25pt, "average grain size from small sections such as this. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 395.93pt, "Local effects are present even in large components, where grains near a surface or side-")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 416.69pt, "wall can be different to internal grains. For example, the surface grains in the Hastelloy-")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 437.33pt, "X sample in Figure 5 are elongated along the radial direction, whereas internal grains ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 458.09pt, "are elongated along the build direction (this is explored further in Reference [12]). In ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 478.75pt, "the interlaboratory comparison study in Reference [1], a major uncertainty component ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 499.51pt, "was related to whether or not participants chose to crop out fine grains near the AM ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 520.15pt, "build surface from the analysis FoV.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#2f5496"))
  #line-at(85.104pt, 549.91pt, "4.2")
  #line-at(113.9pt, 549.91pt, "EBSD analysis parameters  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.104pt, 571.27pt, "Section 2.6 showed that grain sizes measured from EBSD maps acquired at different ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 592.03pt, "step sizes are not directly comparable, even if the boundary threshold angles are the ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 612.67pt, "same, because the high temperature gradients of AM processes can generate ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 633.43pt, "microstructures with sub-grain boundaries and large intragranular misorientations. This ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 654.07pt, "is different to microstructures with fully recrystallised grains separated by high-angle ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 674.86pt, "grain boundaries, where EBSD map step size and grain boundary threshold angle can be ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 695.5pt, "treated as independent parameters. Therefore, grain size should always be reported with ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 716.26pt, "its EBSD map step size and grain boundary threshold angle in such microstructures. ")
]

