#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.104pt, 96.26pt, "There is no objectively optimal value for the threshold angle, but it affects the measured ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 116.9pt, "grain size greatly, and different values can be appropriate for different material types. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 137.66pt, "Identical threshold angles (and step sizes) must be used when comparing EBSD grain ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 158.3pt, "sizes. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 186.98pt, "Since AM microstructures have very wide size distributions, a good grain size spatial ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 207.74pt, "resolution should be balanced with a sufficiently large FoV. The guidance on EBSD ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 228.38pt, "map step size selection in ISO 13067 (around 10 % of the average grain diameter, in this ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 249.14pt, "case minimum Feret diameter) is a good starting point. Pixellation uncertainties in ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 269.78pt, "reconstructing very small individual grains are not important for measurement of ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 290.57pt, "average grain size, as it only affects the small-grains tail of the grain size cumulative ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 311.21pt, "histogram, not the median (Figure 3b). However, AM grain shapes are often non-convex ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 331.97pt, "so using a too-large step size can break up thin sections of large grains. 20 % of the ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 352.61pt, "average grain diameter, recommended in Reference [7], may be appropriate for convex ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 373.37pt, "polygonal grain structures, but is too coarse for resolving AM structures. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 402.05pt, "The FoV size should be as large as practical and follow the aspect ratio of the grains, ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 422.69pt, "since a small FoV increases the random sampling uncertainty (Section 2.5) which ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 443.45pt, "dominates the combined uncertainty (Table 2). 20 grain lengths per FoV were required ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 464.09pt, "to achieve a sampling uncertainty of about 10 %. A lower bound for the sampling ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 484.87pt, "uncertainty contribution was approximated using a bootstrap model for this work, and ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 505.51pt, "should be determined by sampling non-overlapping fields of view in a real ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 526.27pt, "measurement.  ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 554.95pt, "A second effect of a too-small FoV is that a larger proportion grains are grains will cut ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 575.59pt, "the map edge and be excluded (Section 2.4). In a small FoV, few or no grains contribute ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 596.35pt, "to the large-grained end of the indexed-area-weighted cumulative histogram, so the ")
